<Ui>
    <!--
        benilla's Honor tab: our own build of the 1.12 `HonorFrame`, the fifth and last page of the
        character window. The reference FrameXML `HonorFrame.{xml,lua}` + `HonorFrameTemplates.xml`
        are the SPECIFICATION throughout (cites read `ref l.N` into `HonorFrame.xml`, `ref-lua l.N`
        into `HonorFrame.lua`, `ref-tpl l.N` into `HonorFrameTemplates.xml`); every geometry number
        and art path below is QUOTED from them, because those are the client's facts and there is no
        other value they could take. The Lua is OURS, written against that specification and onto
        the PvP/honor Era API (`benilla-ui/src/script/pvp.rs` and its honor siblings). Frame names
        are the reference's, because a frame name is the addon-visible contract and not ours to
        vary.

        WHAT THE PAGE IS. A rank block at the top (badge, rank title, "(Rank N)", and the progress
        bar that says how far through the rank you are), then five labelled sections of counters:
        Today, Yesterday, This Week, Last Week, Lifetime.

        A CHILD PAGE of `CharacterFrame` (ref l.5: `parent="CharacterFrame"`, `id="5"`), shown and
        hidden by `CharacterFrame_ShowSubFrame` — but a SEPARATE top-level file, the
        SkillFrame.xml / PetPaperDollFrame.xml / ReputationFrame.xml shape: this engine reads no XML
        `parent=` attribute (only physical `<Frames>` nesting creates a real parent), so the page is
        pinned to `CharacterFrame`'s own TOPLEFT by NAME and loaded right after its siblings so it
        paints on top (later file = higher z). Joining `BENILLA_CHARACTERFRAME_SUBFRAMES` as entry 5
        is all it has to do to be closed by `CharacterFrame_OnHide`'s hide-loop.

        THE TAB ROW IS NOW WHOLE. Four of the reference's five tabs shipped before this page and
        each sat at the reference's own number; this is the fifth, so the row and
        `BENILLA_CHARACTERFRAME_SUBFRAMES` are the real client's, entire, with nothing left past
        the end. The invariant CharacterFrame.xml states — a page's `id=` IS its slot in the tab
        row, and the roster is the same 1:1 mapping written the other way round — is pinned by
        `character_tests.rs`, whose loop now runs the full five.

        NO OWN PORTRAIT: the "General" quadrant art below carries the portrait-ring cutout every
        PaperDollInfoFrame page shares, so `CharacterFrame`'s portrait/name/level (drawn underneath)
        shows through. ReputationFrame.xml's and SkillFrame.xml's note, unchanged.

        THE TWO SHARED PAINTERS. `BenillaHonorPane_SetRows` and `BenillaHonorPane_SetRank` are
        written unit-first and pane-first because the INSPECT window's honor page (InspectFrame.xml)
        paints the identical twelve rows and the identical rank block from a foreign player's
        numbers. The reference keeps two full copies of both, one per pane, differing only in the
        frame prefix and the unit token; we keep one. The row templates below are shared the same
        way — the reference shares those too (its `HonorFrameTemplates.xml` is `<Include>`d by
        `HonorFrame.xml` and inherited by `Blizzard_InspectUI`), which is why this file must load
        BEFORE `InspectFrame.xml` in `benilla.toc`.

        DEVIATIONS (each re-cited at its own site below):
          1. THE GLOBALSTRINGS ARE NAMED, NEVER COPIED. Older windows here carry an
             `X = X or "<the enUS text>"` fallback so a chainless harness still paints words; this
             page ships no such copy for its own labels, because three of them are the
             `NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_*` paragraphs and shipping Blizzard's prose was what decision 1234
             forbade (retired by 1602; the lookup stays because it is the better design). The section labels reach the player's own strings through the loader's
             `text=` resolution (a `text=` attribute is a GlobalStrings LOOKUP, `loader::resolve_text`);
             the row labels and tooltip bodies reach them as plain globals. A harness with no
             install behind it therefore paints a key where a label goes and raises if you hover a
             row — the honest face of "these words live in the install, not in our tree". The ONE
             exception is `PLAYER_LEVEL`, a format template rather than prose, declared here with
             the same guarded fallback CharacterFrame.xml and InspectFrame.xml already carry and
             for their stated reason: load order must not be a hidden dependency between windows.
          2. THE NEWBIE TIPS GO THROUGH THE HOUSE HELPER. All five hover sites call
             `BenillaTooltip_AddNewbieTipFor` (GameTooltip.xml), so they obey `SHOW_NEWBIE_TIPS` —
             the "Show Detailed Tooltips" box our own OptionsFrame ships — like every other newbie
             tip in this UI. The reference's row template does the same thing by hand (ref-tpl
             l.8-16) but ignores the CVar, and its rank/bar pair (ref l.324-333, l.352-361) ignores
             it AND anchors beside the widget instead of at the screen corner. Detailed tips are ON
             by default (1.12's own default, GameTooltip.xml), so the default look is the
             reference's; the difference is that a player who turned them off is obeyed here.
          3. THE ROW TEMPLATES ARE THE REFERENCE'S FIVE, under the reference's five names, but the
             base carries everything that can be shared — the strip's size and the whole hover —
             and the leaves carry only the two FontStrings. They cannot carry less: this engine's
             `inherits=` concatenates children instead of merging them by name
             (`framexml::merge`), so a leaf that re-declared an inherited region would end up with
             two of it. That constraint is stated at the template rather than discovered later.
          4. THE LEVEL LINE PAINTS ITSELF. `HonorLevelText` is the reference's own FontString, and
             the reference fills it from the PAPER DOLL's setter — `PaperDollFrame_SetLevel` writes
             `CharacterLevelText` and then, in its own words, sets it "for the honor frame while we
             at it" (ref PaperDollFrame.lua:100-104). That is a hidden page reaching across a file
             boundary into another hidden page. The string belongs to THIS page, so the write does
             too: this file registers `UNIT_LEVEL` itself and paints its own line.
          5. `HonorGuildText` (ref l.68-76) IS DROPPED, with its `CharacterGuildText` twin — decision
             0208's own named deferral, restated in CharacterFrame.xml: the guild line needs a guild
             query this window has never made, and the honor page's copy is the same line.
          6. THE RANK TITLE'S XML `y` IS THE ONE THE PAGE ACTUALLY WEARS. The reference anchors
             `HonorFrameCurrentPVPTitle` at TOP (0,-87) and then re-points it every repaint at
             (…,-83) (ref l.127-134 vs ref-lua l.76); its inspect twin anchors at -81 and re-points
             at -83 the same way. The XML value is unobservable — nothing paints before the first
             repaint — so the two contradictory numbers are collapsed to the one that survives.
          7. `enableMouse="true"` on the StatusBar and on the two hover frames. In this engine only
             Button/CheckButton/EditBox/ScrollingMessageFrame are mouse-enabled by construction
             (`widget/mod.rs`; UnitFrames.xml, CharacterFrame.xml and ReputationFrame.xml all carry
             the same note), so a Frame with an `<OnEnter>` needs the attribute or the handler
             silently never fires. This REPRODUCES the reference's behaviour rather than departing
             from it.
    -->
    <Script><![CDATA[
        -- ── The one string this file spells out ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
        -- A format TEMPLATE, not prose, and declared here rather than borrowed from
        -- CharacterFrame.xml's identical line for that file's own stated reason: load order must
        -- not be a hidden dependency between two windows. Everything else this page says comes out
        -- of the player's own GlobalStrings.lua (this file's header, deviation 1).
        PLAYER_LEVEL = PLAYER_LEVEL or "Level %d %s %s"

        -- ── The row: two strings and a hover ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        -- A row remembers the pair its hover shows: its own label as the tip's title, and the
        -- matching detailed explanation as the body. Both are the player's strings, passed in by
        -- the instance below rather than baked into a template, so the five leaf templates differ
        -- only in what they LOOK like.
        function BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(row, title, detail)
            row.title = title
            row.detail = detail
        end

        -- The house newbie tip (this file's header, deviation 2): label in highlight white, the
        -- explanation wrapped under it in NORMAL_FONT_COLOR, both only when the player has detailed
        -- tips on.
        function BenillaHonorRow_OnEnter(row)
            BenillaTooltip_AddNewbieTipFor(row, row.title,
                HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.r, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.g, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.b, row.detail)
        end

        -- ── The two painters both honor panes share (ref-lua l.19-77 and its inspect twin) ───────

        -- Paint a set of counters. `pane` is the owning frame's name, `values` a
        -- `{ [rowSuffix] = number }` table: each row frame is `<pane><suffix>` and its figure lives
        -- in that frame's own `$parentValue` string, so a repaint is a table walk rather than
        -- twelve hand-written assignments. Rows are looked up by name and skipped when absent,
        -- which is what lets a caller paint a SUBSET (the character pane repaints its weekly
        -- sections on a different trigger from its session ones — see HonorFrame_Update).
        --
        -- A nil figure is likewise not painted, and that falls out of Lua rather than being coded:
        -- a nil value never enters the table at all, so `pairs` cannot reach it. That is the
        -- behaviour we want on the inspect side, where the whole twelve-wide read answers with
        -- NOTHING until the server's reply lands — better a row that has not been filled in yet
        -- than a row confidently showing a zero nobody sent.
        function BenillaHonorPane_SetRows(pane, values)
            for suffix, value in pairs(values) do
                local figure = getglobal(pane .. suffix .. "Value")
                if figure then
                    figure:SetText(value)
                end
            end
        end

        -- Paint the rank block: the badge, the rank's title, the "(Rank N)" that trails it, and the
        -- progress bar under both. `rank` is the unit's rank as the engine reports it, `progress`
        -- the 0..1 position within that rank, `unit` the token whose faction colours the bar.
        --
        -- Called with ("HonorFrame", …, "player") by the page below and with
        -- ("BenillaInspectHonorFrame", …, the inspected token) by InspectFrame.xml.
        function BenillaHonorPane_SetRank(pane, rank, progress, unit)
            local rankName, rankNumber = GetPVPRankInfo(rank)
            local title = getglobal(pane .. "CurrentPVPTitle")
            local ordinal = getglobal(pane .. "CurrentPVPRank")

            -- THE NIL IS THE MECHANISM, not an edge case. The rank names are GlobalStrings keyed
            -- `PVP_RANK_<rank>_<team>` and there is no `PVP_RANK_0_*`: rank 0 means "no rank at
            -- all", which is every character who has never taken an honorable kill. So the name
            -- comes back nil there and the pane says NONE. The NUMBER is not guarded, and that is
            -- deliberate too: it is a rank arithmetic result, never absent, and swallowing a nil
            -- would hide a broken binding instead of reporting it.
            title:SetText(rankName or NONE)
            -- `RANK or ""` rather than a bare concat: a harness with no install behind it has no
            -- GlobalStrings, and taking the pane down over a missing label helps nobody.
            ordinal:SetText("(" .. (RANK or "") .. " " .. rankNumber .. ")")

            -- The badge is built HERE, not by the engine: the file name is the rank number padded
            -- to two digits, and it is shown only above zero. Verified against the patch chain this
            -- session: `PvPRank01`..`PvPRank15` exist and `PvPRank00`/`16`/`17`/`18`/`19` do not,
            -- which is exactly the range the arithmetic can produce (the fourteen player ranks plus
            -- the PvP-leader one); zero and the four negative ranks have no badge to show.
            local badge = getglobal(pane .. "PvPIcon")
            if rankNumber > 0 then
                badge:SetTexture(format("Interface\\PvPRankBadges\\PvPRank%02d", rankNumber))
                badge:Show()
            else
                badge:Hide()
            end

            -- The bar wears the unit's own faction: Alliance navy, everything else Horde red. The
            -- fork is a positive test for Alliance with an `else`, not a two-sided test, so a unit
            -- whose faction group reads nil (a race the app has no snapshot for yet — ui_unit.rs's
            -- own note) still gets a painted bar rather than a bare trough.
            local bar = getglobal(pane .. "ProgressBar")
            if UnitFactionGroup(unit) == "Alliance" then
                bar:SetStatusBarColor(0.05, 0.15, 0.36)
            else
                bar:SetStatusBarColor(0.63, 0.09, 0.09)
            end
            bar:SetValue(progress)

            -- Re-centre the PAIR, not the title. The title is centred on the pane and then shifted
            -- left by half the width of the "(Rank N)" that trails it, so title and ordinal read as
            -- one centred phrase. It has to be redone on every repaint because that width moves
            -- with the number and with the locale.
            title:SetPoint("TOP", pane, "TOP", -ordinal:GetWidth() / 2, -83)
        end

        -- ── The character window's honor page (ref-lua l.1-13) ───────────────────────────────────

        -- The two PvP events plus world entry, and one of our own. The SPLIT between them is the
        -- reference's and it is deliberate: the weekly sections (yesterday, this week, last week)
        -- only move at the server's weekly maintenance, so nothing carries them mid-session and
        -- re-reading them per kill would be waste. World entry is the flag that says "repaint those
        -- too". `UNIT_LEVEL` is ours (this file's header, deviation 4).
        function HonorFrame_OnLoad(frame)
            frame:RegisterEvent("PLAYER_PVP_KILLS_CHANGED")
            frame:RegisterEvent("PLAYER_PVP_RANK_CHANGED")
            frame:RegisterEvent("PLAYER_ENTERING_WORLD")
            frame:RegisterEvent("UNIT_LEVEL")
        end

        function HonorFrame_OnEvent(event)
            if event == "UNIT_LEVEL" then
                if arg1 == "player" then
                    HonorFrame_SetLevel()
                end
                return
            end
            HonorFrame_Update(event == "PLAYER_ENTERING_WORLD")
        end

        -- The level/race/class line under the window's name (this file's header, deviation 4). The
        -- "?" placeholders match CharacterFrame.xml's and exist for its reason: UnitRace/UnitClass
        -- read nil for the one tick before the app's first player snapshot lands, and an unguarded
        -- format() errors on that nil.
        function HonorFrame_SetLevel()
            getglobal("HonorLevelText"):SetText(
                format(PLAYER_LEVEL, UnitLevel("player"), UnitRace("player") or "?", UnitClass("player") or "?"))
        end

        -- `weekly` repaints the three sections that only move at the weekly reset; everything else
        -- is repainted on every call. Note which return each figure comes from — the four stat
        -- readers do NOT have the same shape, and that is the server's doing, not a slip:
        -- yesterday and last week report dishonorable kills the pane does not show, and only last
        -- week carries a standing.
        function HonorFrame_Update(weekly)
            if weekly then
                local yesterdayHK, _, yesterdayHonor = GetPVPYesterdayStats()
                local thisWeekHK, thisWeekHonor = GetPVPThisWeekStats()
                local lastWeekHK, _, lastWeekHonor, standing = GetPVPLastWeekStats()
                BenillaHonorPane_SetRows("HonorFrame", {
                    YesterdayHK = yesterdayHK,
                    YesterdayContribution = yesterdayHonor,
                    ThisWeekHK = thisWeekHK,
                    ThisWeekContribution = thisWeekHonor,
                    LastWeekHK = lastWeekHK,
                    LastWeekContribution = lastWeekHonor,
                    LastWeekStanding = standing,
                })
            end

            local sessionHK, sessionDK = GetPVPSessionStats()
            local lifetimeHK, lifetimeDK, highestRank = GetPVPLifetimeStats()
            -- The highest rank EVER held, which is a different field from the current one and moves
            -- only upward; its name falls back to NONE on the same nil as the live rank's.
            local highestName = GetPVPRankInfo(highestRank)
            BenillaHonorPane_SetRows("HonorFrame", {
                CurrentHK = sessionHK,
                CurrentDK = sessionDK,
                LifeTimeHK = lifetimeHK,
                LifeTimeDK = lifetimeDK,
                LifeTimeRank = highestName or NONE,
            })

            BenillaHonorPane_SetRank("HonorFrame", UnitPVPRank("player"), GetPVPRankProgress(), "player")
            HonorFrame_SetLevel()
        end
    ]]></Script>

    <!--
        The row's SHARED half (ref-tpl l.3-18): a 278x12 strip and the hover that explains it. The
        five leaves below add the two FontStrings, because this engine's `inherits=` CONCATENATES a
        template's children onto the inheriting node's rather than merging them by name
        (`framexml::merge`) — so a leaf that re-declared `$parentText` would get TWO of them, both
        drawn, sharing one name. Everything that can be hoisted is hoisted; the regions cannot be.

        `enableMouse` is required for the hover to fire at all (this file's header, deviation 7).
    -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true" enableMouse="true">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="278" y="12"/></Size>
        <Scripts>
            <OnEnter>BenillaHonorRow_OnEnter(self)</OnEnter>
            <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!--
        The five leaves (ref-tpl l.19-148). Each is the same pair of strings in the same two places
        and differs in exactly two things: WHICH label it carries, and what colour its figure is.
        The label is the player's own GlobalStrings key, resolved by the loader (a `text=` attribute
        is a lookup, not a literal); the figure's colour rides a font object rather than a colour
        call, which is how every other coloured string in this UI is painted.

        The value's `RIGHT` anchored to the row's own `LEFT` plus 278 is the reference's spelling of
        "right-aligned at the row's right edge", quoted rather than restated as an anchor to RIGHT.

        Honorable kills: GREEN.
    -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate" inherits="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="HONORABLE_KILLS" justifyH="LEFT">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="$parentValue" inherits="GameFontGreenSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="278" y="1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(self, HONORABLE_KILLS, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_HONORABLE_KILLS)</OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- Dishonorable kills: RED (ref-tpl l.45-70), because a dishonorable kill costs you rank —
         the colour is the pane telling you which way the number moves you. -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameDKButtonTemplate" inherits="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="DISHONORABLE_KILLS" justifyH="LEFT">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="$parentValue" inherits="GameFontRedSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="278" y="1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(self, DISHONORABLE_KILLS, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_DISHONORABLE_KILLS)</OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- Honor earned in the period (ref-tpl l.71-96). The key is HONOR_CONTRIBUTION_POINTS and the
         string it holds in 1.12 is the single word "Honor" — the key is the older name for the same
         figure, kept because it is the key the player's own string table actually has. -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameContributionButtonTemplate" inherits="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="HONOR_CONTRIBUTION_POINTS" justifyH="LEFT">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="$parentValue" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="278" y="1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(self, HONOR_CONTRIBUTION_POINTS, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_HONOR_CONTRIBUTION_POINTS)</OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- Last week's standing (ref-tpl l.97-122): where you placed against everyone on your own
         side. Only the Last Week section has one — it is a ranking of a finished week. -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameStandingButtonTemplate" inherits="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="HONOR_STANDING" justifyH="LEFT">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="$parentValue" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="278" y="1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(self, HONOR_STANDING, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_HONOR_STANDING)</OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- The highest rank ever held (ref-tpl l.123-148). Its label and its TIP TITLE deliberately
         differ, and that is the reference's own choice, not a slip: the row is labelled "Highest
         Rank" because that is what the figure is, while the hover is titled "Rank" because the
         paragraph under it explains ranking in general. -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrameRankButtonTemplate" inherits="HonorFrameTextButtonTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="HONOR_HIGHEST_RANK" justifyH="LEFT">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="$parentValue" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="278" y="1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>BenillaHonorRow_OnLoad(self, RANK, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_RANK)</OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- id=5, the reference's own (ref l.5): a page's id is the slot it occupies in the WINDOW'S
         OWN tab row, and ToggleCharacter selects the tab from it. `character_tests.rs` pins every
         page's id against its slot in BENILLA_CHARACTERFRAME_SUBFRAMES, and with this page the two
         cover the real client's whole row. -->
    <Frame name="HonorFrame" enableMouse="true" parent="CharacterFrame" hidden="true" id="5">
        <!-- The real 384x512 page. The reference's `setAllPoints="true"` needs the true parenting
             this engine's cross-file `parent=` does not give (this file's header comment), so the
             page is sized and pinned to CharacterFrame's TOPLEFT by name — ReputationFrame.xml,
             SkillFrame.xml and PetPaperDollFrame.xml all ship the identical substitute. -->
        <Size><AbsDimension x="384" y="512"/></Size>
        <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="CharacterFrame" relativePoint="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
        <HitRectInsets><AbsInset left="0" right="30" top="0" bottom="75"/></HitRectInsets>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <!-- The four quadrant slabs (ref l.10-58), at the reference's own four TOPLEFT
                     offsets. The "General" set is the variant every PaperDollInfoFrame page shares
                     and carries the portrait-ring cutout (this file's header comment). -->
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-General-TopLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="2" y="-1"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-General-TopRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="258" y="-1"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-General-BottomLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="2" y="-257"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-General-BottomRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="258" y="-257"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <!-- The level/race/class line, under the window's own name (ref l.59-67). This page
                     is the only sibling of the paper doll that carries one; the guild line beside
                     it in the reference is dropped with its twin (header deviation 5). The
                     reference ships a "Level level race class" placeholder in the attribute, which
                     only shows on a page that has not been painted — and this one is painted before
                     it can be seen, so the placeholder is dropped (the ReputationFrame.xml
                     precedent for the reference's own dead placeholders). -->
                <FontString name="HonorLevelText" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP" relativeTo="CharacterNameText" relativePoint="BOTTOM"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-6"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
            <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                <!-- The pane's OWN art, over the shared background: the rank plaque and the five
                     section plates (ref l.79-126). Four slabs again, but not the same four sizes —
                     the bottom pair is 128 tall, not 256. -->
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-Honor-TopLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="22" y="-69"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-Honor-TopRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="275" y="-69"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-Honor-BottomLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="128"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="22" y="-325"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-Honor-BottomRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="128"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="275" y="-325"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <!-- The rank title and the "(Rank N)" beside it (ref l.127-144). The title's y is
                     the repaint's, not the XML's (header deviation 6); the pair is re-centred
                     together on every repaint by BenillaHonorPane_SetRank. -->
                <FontString name="HonorFrameCurrentPVPTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOP"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-83"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="HonorFrameCurrentPVPRank" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="LEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentPVPTitle" relativePoint="RIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="5" y="-1"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <!-- The five section headings (ref l.145-189), each chained off the last. The gaps
                     are NOT uniform (41/43/42/64) because each section holds a different number of
                     rows; the 64 before Lifetime is the three-row Last Week section above it. Each
                     `text=` is the player's own GlobalStrings key, resolved by the loader — note
                     the first one reads "Today" in 1.12, not "This Session". -->
                <FontString name="HonorFrameCurrentSessionTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="HONOR_THIS_SESSION">
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="36" y="-112"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="HonorFrameYesterdayTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="HONOR_YESTERDAY">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentSessionTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-41"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="HonorFrameThisWeekTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="HONOR_THISWEEK">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameYesterdayTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-43"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="HonorFrameLastWeekTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="HONOR_LASTWEEK">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameThisWeekTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-42"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="HonorFrameLifeTimeTitle" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="HONOR_LIFETIME">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLastWeekTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-64"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
            <Layer level="OVERLAY">
                <!-- The rank badge, left of the title (ref l.191-204). No `file=` here: the path is
                     built per rank at repaint time (BenillaHonorPane_SetRank), and there is no
                     badge at all below rank 1 — an unpainted texture is an empty one. -->
                <Texture name="HonorFramePvPIcon">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="24" y="24"/></Size>
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentPVPTitle" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-5" y="0"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </Texture>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Frames>
            <!-- THE TWELVE ROWS (ref l.207-314), section by section. Each section's first row
                 hangs off its heading's BOTTOMLEFT indented 10, and each following row chains off
                 the one above it. The row SET per section is the shape of the data, not a layout
                 choice: Today is the only section that shows dishonorable kills beside honorable
                 ones without an honor figure (honor for today is not banked yet), the two middle
                 sections trade the dishonorable count for the honor earned, Last Week adds the
                 standing that closed it, and Lifetime trades honor for the highest rank ever held.

                 Today. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameCurrentHK" inherits="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentSessionTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="10" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameCurrentDK" inherits="HonorFrameDKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentHK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-2"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>

            <!-- Yesterday. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameYesterdayHK" inherits="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameYesterdayTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="10" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameYesterdayContribution" inherits="HonorFrameContributionButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameYesterdayHK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>

            <!-- This Week. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameThisWeekHK" inherits="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameThisWeekTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="10" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameThisWeekContribution" inherits="HonorFrameContributionButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameThisWeekHK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>

            <!-- Last Week: the only section with three rows, and the only one with a standing. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLastWeekHK" inherits="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLastWeekTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="10" y="-3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLastWeekContribution" inherits="HonorFrameContributionButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLastWeekHK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-2"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLastWeekStanding" inherits="HonorFrameStandingButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLastWeekContribution" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-2"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>

            <!-- Lifetime. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLifeTimeHK" inherits="HonorFrameHKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLifeTimeTitle" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="10" y="-4"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLifeTimeDK" inherits="HonorFrameDKButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLifeTimeHK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-4"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <Frame name="HonorFrameLifeTimeRank" inherits="HonorFrameRankButtonTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="HonorFrameLifeTimeDK" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-4"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>

            <!-- The rank hover (ref l.315-334): an invisible strip spanning badge to ordinal, so
                 the whole "badge Marshal (Rank 10)" phrase explains itself. Two anchors and no
                 width of its own — the LEFT edge is the badge's, the RIGHT edge the ordinal's, so
                 the strip re-fits itself when either moves. -->
            <Frame name="HonorFrameRankButton" enableMouse="true">
                <Size><AbsDimension x="10" y="24"/></Size>
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="LEFT" relativeTo="HonorFramePvPIcon"/>
                    <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeTo="HonorFrameCurrentPVPRank"/>
                </Anchors>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnEnter>BenillaTooltip_AddNewbieTipFor(self, RANK, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.r, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.g, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.b, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_RANK)</OnEnter>
                    <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
                </Scripts>
            </Frame>

            <!-- The progress bar (ref l.335-370): how far through the current rank you are, 0..1.
                 It reuses the skills bar's own fill art, which is the reference's choice.

                 The two frame-level drops are the reference's and they are the mechanism, not
                 decoration. The bar's fill is a BORDER-layer texture on a child frame, and the
                 pane's plaque art is ARTWORK on the pane itself: at the child's natural level the
                 fill would sort above the plaque and paint over the socket cut for it. Dropping the
                 bar to the pane's own level puts the two in one bucket, where the draw layer
                 decides and ARTWORK wins. The hover frame inside it drops again for the same
                 reason, and because it carries no art its level only decides where a mouse lands. -->
            <StatusBar name="HonorFrameProgressBar" enableMouse="true" drawLayer="BORDER" minValue="0" maxValue="1" defaultValue="0">
                <Size><AbsDimension x="315" y="29"/></Size>
                <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="22" y="-77"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                <Frames>
                    <Frame name="HonorFrameProgressButton" enableMouse="true" setAllPoints="true">
                        <Scripts>
                            <OnLoad>self:SetFrameLevel(self:GetFrameLevel() - 1)</OnLoad>
                            <OnEnter>BenillaTooltip_AddNewbieTipFor(self, RANK_POSITION, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.r, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.g, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.b, NEWBIE_TOOLTIP_RANK_POSITION)</OnEnter>
                            <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
                        </Scripts>
                    </Frame>
                </Frames>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnLoad>self:SetFrameLevel(self:GetFrameLevel() - 1)</OnLoad>
                </Scripts>
                <BarTexture name="$parentBar" file="Interface\PaperDollInfoFrame\UI-Character-Skills-Bar"/>
            </StatusBar>
        </Frames>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>HonorFrame_OnLoad(self)</OnLoad>
            <OnEvent>HonorFrame_OnEvent(event)</OnEvent>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>
</Ui>
